KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ thrown move-only exception carries its value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [except.throw]/3 + [class.copy.elision]/3: the exception object of a
throw is initialized from the operand; for a prvalue operand this is direct
initialization (guaranteed copy elision) or, failing that, a move.  Throwing an
object of a move-only class is well-formed.

CBMC constructs the thrown exception object with the class's copy constructor,
which for a move-only class (Ex here, whose member M has a deleted copy
constructor and a user-provided move constructor) is deleted -- so the throw is
dropped with a CONVERSION ERROR ("found no match for symbol 'Ex'").  CBMC does
handle throw/catch of a copyable class, so the gap is specifically the
copy-vs-move/elision choice for the exception object.

Remaining cause of the enable_if_t<false> failure when compiling CBMC's own
parse_options.cpp with goto-cc (it throws exceptions transitively holding a
move-only, std::unique_ptr-backed ui_message_handlert).  The defaulted
move-constructor half is fixed (see cpp11_defaulted_move_ctor_member).  Flip to
CORE once the exception-object construction moves/elides from a prvalue operand
rather than requiring a copy constructor.
